#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script --quiet
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.12"
# dependencies = ["tyro"]  # diffview's too, whose module this loads
# ///
"""The container hub: one local server for every page an agent opens, and the one hub tab that shows them, a unit per orchestrator.

`claude-browser` hands every page here. A unit is one orchestrator session: a page goes to the unit of the
session that opened it ($MX_ORIGIN_SESSION, as claude-browser passes it), a session page to its own session's,
and a page no session opened to the loose pages. A session is a unit once it has opened a page here. A unit is
labelled by its session's title (its session page's heading, else the title Claude Code keeps in the session's
transcript, the user's from /rename over its own, else its name in Claude Code's session registry)
with its tree beside it: the tree of the ticket the session last committed on (its `Session:` trailers), else
of the ticket a page of it names (`agent/show/<slug>/`, `agent/prototypes/<slug>/`, `agent/diffviews/<slug>.html`,
`agent/tickets/<slug>.md`), and its short id: its name in the registry while it runs, else the first 8 characters
of its id.

The hub tab (`/`) shows every unit, switched between on its rail. The rail numbers the units whose sessions run
by recency: 1 is the session that most recently finished a turn, busy sessions after idle ones. A turn's end is
when the session's page was last rendered, or when the registry (`~/.claude*/sessions/<pid>.json`) last marked
the session idle. A unit whose session's process has exited leaves the numbers for the ended list, and gets one
again when the session is resumed; one dismissed with its ✕ is out of them until its session next finishes a
turn. The loose pages, of no session, have no number. A unit goes once its tree is retired, or, with no tree,
once every page of it is deleted.

The rail's search box (ctrl+alt+f) finds a unit, ended ones included, as fzf finds a line of its title, short id and
session id, a prefix of either id first, and enter goes to the one picked.

A unit's account is the Claude Code config dir its session runs under: the one its registry file sits in while it
runs, else the one its transcript (`~/.claude*/projects/<project>/<session id>.jsonl`) sits in. The rail ticks
accounts on and off, each browser profile keeping its own ticks, and leaves an unticked account's units out of
everything but the hub's state.

Membership, minimized pages, visits and dismissals live in one JSON file per unit under --state; everything else is
read from disk on every request, so the hub's state can go and only those marks go with it. What it read of Claude
Code's transcripts, which only grow, it keeps in memory and reads on from.

Every page has a lane, its level of abstraction, from the highest to the lowest: session, concept, output, code. A
page's lane follows from what it is: a session page goes to session, a diffview page to code, an image to concept,
a page on another origin to output, any other page in a show directory to concept, and anything else to output. An
agent's `lane` overrides that, and holds until another names a lane. The hub tab shows a unit's session page in a
sidebar of its own, its concept and output pages in a pane on the left, and its code in a pane on the right.

A page on another origin (a running app, a local server) is framed in its pane where it allows framing, and the hub
tab's keys and link handling do not reach inside it. A page refuses framing by its X-Frame-Options, or by a
Content-Security-Policy whose frame-ancestors leaves out http://127.0.0.1:<port>. Such a page is a member all the
same, though laid out nowhere. Its unit's session page lists it, and a click on its line, or ctrl+alt+shift+w, opens
it as a browser tab. So does a click on a link to it in the hub tab; an agent opening it opens no tab. The hub asks
the page at every open, and frames one that does not answer.

A unit's session page is the list of its pages: the artefacts the page's column links, then every page of the unit
no turn links, which the hub tab adds at the column's end. The hub tab marks each line open, minimized or not opened
yet. A page the user closes is minimized: it stays a member, and comes back from its line in that list, or when an
agent opens it again. A unit whose session has rendered no session page yet, and the loose pages, have a stand-in for
one, served by the hub. It is a page with an empty column, which the hub tab fills the same way. It goes once
the session page exists.

The board (a tracker's `agent/board.html`) is no page of a unit. Handed to the hub, by an agent or by a link clicked
in a page, it opens over the hub tab, as ctrl+alt+b does there, and a unit never lists it. Over a unit, the board is
its tree's. It marks the tickets that are not done among those the session claimed or committed on (its `Session:`
trailers), and scrolls the first of them into view.

Pages are served from here (`/f/<absolute path>`), so the hub tab shares their origin and reaches into them:
their links and keys. A diffview page served here saves its comments through diffview's own code;
`diffview --open`, `--watch` and `--serve` hand their pages here to be served. Worker reports and diffviews of
nothing but ticket files are for agents and never open.

A unit's session page carries an input box that types into the session's tmux pane, the one the registry names,
so the user answers a turn where they read it, and the terminal and the hub show one conversation. The text goes in
as a paste followed by Enter, as if typed there. Nothing goes to a session blocked in its terminal (the registry's
`waiting`: a permission prompt, a question, a dialog), to one that runs in no tmux pane, or to a pane that no longer
holds the session's process. A file pasted or dropped into the box is saved under the cache's `container-hub/drops/`
and its path typed in its place. Both routes want the token the hub hands to a top-level load of / alone, so a page
the hub serves cannot type into a session by fetching them. It shares the tab's origin all the same, so a page that
scripts the tab itself is not stopped; only a page served from another origin would be.

The line the Stop hook prints under a finished turn links the hub tab at that session's unit, /u/<session id> on
this hub (http://127.0.0.1:8377/u/<session id> on the default port), wherever the hub answers; where none does,
the session page's file:// link.

The rail shows what the statusline shows: each running session's context use, and each account's 5h and weekly
usage, read from what the statusline keeps under `~/.cache/claude-statusline/`. A session with no file there shows
none.

    GET  /                    the hub tab
    GET  /u/<key>             the hub tab at a unit: a session id, or `loose`. A container view's old address,
                              /c/<key>, is sent to the hub tab.
    GET  /events              server-sent events: something in the hub changed. One stream per hub tab.
    GET  /pages/<key>         a unit's stand-in for its session page, while it has none.
    POST /api/open            {"target", "session"?, "cwd"?, "unit"?, "title"?, "by"?, "lane"?}: a path, file:// URL or
                              URL becomes a member of its unit, or comes forward again if it is one. Every answer
                              carries "say", the line the caller prints, and "open", the absolute URL the caller
                              opens in the browser, or null to open nothing. Beside them: {"unit", "id", "action",
                              "view", "launch"} for a member, where launch says no hub tab listens and open is the
                              hub tab at the member; {"skipped": why} for an agent's page; {"tab": why} for a link
                              clicked in the hub tab to a page that refuses framing, which joins no unit, and open
                              is that URL as given; {"board": its url, "launch"} for a board, which opens over the
                              hub tab. "unit" is the hub tab's own: the unit a link was clicked in.
    POST /api/close           {"unit", "id"}: minimize a member; it stays a member, and an open brings it back.
    POST /api/visit           {"unit"}: the user looked at a unit; what was new there is not.
    POST /api/dismiss         {"unit"}: out of the numbers until its session next finishes a turn.
    POST /api/type            {"unit", "text"}, from the hub tab with its X-Hub-Token: type the text into the unit's
                              session and press Enter. 409 {"error": why} where nothing may be typed.
    POST /api/drop?unit=&name=   a file's bytes, from the hub tab with its X-Hub-Token: saved, answers {"path"}.
    POST /api/serve           {"path"}: from a local tool only, serve a page with what sits beside it, or a
                              directory with everything under it, without making it a member. Answers {"url"}.
    GET  /api/hub             {"units": [...], "usage": [...]}: every unit, the numbered ones
                              first in the rail's order, then the dismissed, then the ended, each the latest turn
                              first; and each account's 5h and weekly usage, as the statusline last had them.
    GET  /api/u/<key>         one unit: {"unit", "session", "account", "title", "short", "tree", "repo", "state", "waiting_for",
                              "pane", "context" (its running session's context use, as the statusline last had it),
                              "last", "number", "board", "worked_on" (the tickets the board marks),
                              "dismissed", "members": [{"id", "url", "closed", "lane", "tab"?, ...}], "pages",
                              ...}. A member's url is where the hub serves it, relative to here, and its "tab" why it
                              refuses to be framed, where it does. "pages" is every page
                              of the unit as its session page lists it: [{"url", "artefact", "linked", ...}];
                              "session_page" {"url", "id"} where that page is, or its stand-in while it has none.
    GET  /diff?tracker=&ticket=   a ticket's recorded `code@` ranges, rendered by diffview on first open
    GET  /f/<path>            a file under $HOME or the temp dir; a shared directory without an index.html lists
                              its pages
    POST /f/<page>.state.json a diffview page saving its comments
    GET  /f/<page>.watch.json the watch record of a page `diffview --watch` keeps current, with "alive": whether
                              its diffview watcher still runs. A watched page follows its rebuilds by it, and leaves its
                              unit once a repo it watched is gone.
    POST /f/<page>.summary.json  a watched page asking its diffview watcher to write the summary again; 409 with
                              none running
    GET  /.health             {"saves": true}: diffview pages served here can save

Examples:

    container-hub
    container-hub --port 18377 --state /tmp/hub-state
"""

